1
00:04:05,714 --> 00:04:11,714
From 1984 to 1994 a perfect storm of people and
circumstances changed the face of animation forever.

2
00:08:11,428 --> 00:08:17,428
Waking Sleeping Beauty is a documentary film directed by Don Hahn.

3
00:12:17,142 --> 00:12:23,142
By the mid-1980s, the fabled animation studios of
Walt Disney had fallen on hard times.

4
00:16:22,857 --> 00:16:28,857
The artists were polarized between newcomers hungry to innovate
and old timers not yet ready to relinquish control.

5
00:20:28,571 --> 00:20:34,571
These conditions produced a series of box-office flops and pessimistic
forecasts: maybe the best days of animation were over.

6
00:24:34,285 --> 00:24:40,285
Maybe the public didn't care.

7
00:28:40,000 --> 00:28:46,000
Only a miracle or a magic spell could produce a happy ending.

8
00:32:45,714 --> 00:32:51,714
Waking Sleeping Beauty is no fairy tale.

9
00:36:51,428 --> 00:36:57,428
It's the true story of how Disney regained its magic with a staggering output of hits - "Little Mermaid,"
"Beauty and the Beast ," "Aladdin," "The Lion King," and more - over a 10-year period.

10
00:40:57,142 --> 00:41:03,142
Self, played by Don Hahn.

11
00:45:02,857 --> 00:45:08,857
Self, played by Roy Edward Disney.

12
00:49:08,571 --> 00:49:14,571
Self, played by Michael Eisner.

13
00:53:14,285 --> 00:53:20,285
Self, played by Jeffrey Katzenberg.

14
00:57:20,000 --> 00:57:26,000
Self, played by Peter Schneider.

15
01:01:25,714 --> 01:01:31,714
Self - Pumbaa (voice), played by Rob Minkoff.

16
01:05:31,428 --> 01:05:37,428
Waking Sleeping Beauty is a 2009 American documentary film directed by Disney film producer
Don Hahn and produced by Hahn and former Disney executive Peter Schneider.

17
01:09:37,142 --> 01:09:43,142
The film documents the history of Walt Disney Feature Animation from 1984 to 1994,
covering the rise of a period referred to as the Disney Renaissance.

18
01:13:42,857 --> 01:13:48,857
The film uses no new on-camera interviews, instead relying primarily on archival interviews, press kit footage, in-progress and completed footage
from the films being covered, and personal film/videos shot (often against company policy) by the employees of the animation studio.

19
01:17:48,571 --> 01:17:54,571
Waking Sleeping Beauty debuted at the 2009 Telluride Film Festival, and played at film festivals across the country
before its limited theatrical release on March 26, 2010, by Walt Disney Studios Motion Pictures.

20
01:21:54,285 --> 01:22:00,285
The film explores themes of computer animation, struggle for survival,
documentary filmmaking, innovation, challenge for change.
